 -0.01502227783203125, -0.0029754638671875, 0.008148193359375, -0.01457977294921875, 0.024169921875, -0.004650115966796875, -0.03521728515625, -0.024505615234375, -0.00864410400390625, 0.0055999755859375, -0.0308685302734375, 0.01397705078125, -0.0198211669921875, 0.01175689697265625, -0.035919189453125, -0.0032215118408203125, 0.025146484375, 0.01081085205078125, 0.023681640625, -0.03826904296875, -0.00220489501953125, -0.041046142578125, -0.0165252685546875, 0.0012006759643554688, -0.01505279541015625, -0.02838134765625, -0.0174713134765625, 0.01080322265625, 0.009674072265625, -0.0013360977172851562, -0.042755126953125, 0.01617431640625, 0.01088714599609375, 0.00870513916015625, 0.020355224609375, 0.0134735107421875, -0.0105438232421875, 0.01079559326171875, 0.01549530029296875, -0.0204315185546875, 0.015411376953125, 0.0160369873046875, 0.0165252685546875, 0.0309600830078125, 0.01338958740234375, 0.0013599395751953125, -0.0111083984375, -0.003749847412109375, 0.029632568359375, -0.00862884521484375, -0.00894927978515625, 0.01026153564453125, -0.026519775390625, 0.008758544921875, -0.0181121826171875, -0.012908935546875, 0.00824737548828125, 0.02166748046875, -0.0051422119140625, -0.022613525390625, -0.0196533203125, -0.02008056640625, -0.008392333984375, 0.022705078125, -0.011993408203125, 0.0194549560546875, 0.00597381591796875, -0.02667236328125, -0.0008869171142578125, -0.00011372566223144531, 0.0343017578125, 0.005435943603515625, -0.013214111328125, 0.02362060546875, -0.0202484130859375, 0.0010919570922851562, -0.024169921875, -0.01221466064453125, -0.00951385498046875, 0.0033245086669921875, 0.007904052734375, -0.024627685546875, 0.0109710693359375, 0.0097808837890625, 0.0081634521484375, 0.021026611328125, -0.0034542083740234375, -0.053863525390625, -0.02349853515625, 0.02935791015625, -0.01470947265625, 0.02667236328125, -0.03729248046875, 0.005001068115234375, 0.00988006591796875, 0.01233673095703125, 0.004222869873046875, 0.00901031494140625, 0.00408172607421875, 0.01141357421875, 0.029541015625, 0.0243682861328125, 0.005199432373046875, 0.0029239654541015625, -0.02276611328125, -0.0130157470703125, -0.009185791015625, -0.002414703369140625, 0.0017633438110351562, -0.0081329345703125, -0.002628326416015625, 0.0015239715576171875, -0.0248870849609375, -0.0189971923828125, 0.0284423828125, -0.019989013671875, 0.045989990234375, 0.02587890625, 0.0211181640625, 0.0187530517578125, -0.0595703125, -0.0149993896484375, 0.0267333984375, -0.0047760009765625, -0.0007081031799316406, -0.01107025146484375, 0.0170745849609375, 0.0141754150390625, -0.043731689453125, -0.00441741943359375, -0.0035343170166015625, -0.01068878173828125, -0.002513885498046875, 0.0303497314453125, 0.0022373199462890625, -0.01177978515625, -0.0179595947265625, 0.0035610198974609375, 0.005741119384765625, -0.0177001953125, 0.00867462158203125, 0.0012378692626953125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "A volcano is a vent or fissure in the crust of a planetary-mass object that allows hot lava, volcanic ash, and gases to escape from a magma chamber\u2014a reservoir of molten rock beneath the surface. This document provides an overview of the principal volcano types, their tectonic settings, and the geological processes that lead to their formation. It specifically addresses volcanoes at plate boundaries (divergent, convergent, and transform), volcanism in rift zones, intraplate volcanism and hotspots, and how these settings relate to the main volcano types found on Earth.\n\n**Types of Volcanoes**\n\nVolcanoes are commonly classified into three main types based on their shape, eruption style, and composition:\n- **Shield volcanoes** have broad, gently sloping sides and are built by the eruption of low-viscosity basaltic lava. The Hawaiian Islands are classic examples (Macdonald, 1972).\n- **Stratovolcanoes** (or composite volcanoes) are large, steep-sided volcanoes formed by alternating layers of lava flows, ash, and volcanic rocks. They are often associated with explosive eruptions, as seen in Mount Fuji and Mount St. Helens (Francis & Oppenheimer, 2004).\n- **Cinder cone volcanoes** are the smallest type, consisting of steep, conical hills of volcanic fragments (cinders) that accumulate around a single vent. Par\u00edcutin in Mexico is a well-known example (Wood, 1980).\n\n**Volcanoes at Plate Boundaries**\n\nMost volcanoes are associated with tectonic plate boundaries. The majority of Earth's plate boundaries are underwater, and about 80% of the world\u2019s active volcanoes are found beneath the oceans, particularly along mid-ocean ridges (IAVCEI, https://www.iavceivolcano.org/). For example, the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is a divergent plate boundary, where tectonic plates move apart and magma rises to create new crust. At these boundaries, magma is typically basaltic\u2014low in silica and dissolved gas\u2014which results in relatively gentle, non-explosive eruptions.\n\nAt convergent plate boundaries, plates move toward each other, and one plate is forced beneath another in a process called subduction. This process leads to the formation of volcanoes that are often highly explosive, as seen in the Pacific Ring of Fire (Simkin & Siebert, 1994). The subducting plate carries water and sediments into the mantle, lowering the melting point of the overlying mantle wedge (the region of mantle above the subducting plate), and producing magma that is richer in silica and gas. This magma is more viscous and traps gases more easily, leading to explosive eruptions, in contrast to the gentler eruptions at divergent boundaries.\n\n**Volcanoes in Rift Zones**\n\nVolcanoes can also form in regions where the Earth's crust is stretching and thinning, even if these areas are not located at plate boundaries. Examples include the East African Rift, where the African plate is splitting into two and creating a zone of active volcanism; the Wells Gray-Clearwater volcanic field in Canada, associated with continental rifting and crustal extension; and the Rio Grande rift in North America (Ebinger, 2005; Hickson & Souther, 1984). In the East African Rift, large shield volcanoes and fissure eruptions are common due to extensive crustal thinning and upwelling of basaltic magma. The Wells Gray-Clearwater field, by contrast, is characterized by smaller cinder cones and lava flows, reflecting more localized extension and variable magma supply. The Rio Grande rift displays a mix of volcanic types, influenced by both the degree of extension and the composition of the underlying crust. Thus, differences in local geology, magma composition, and the extent of crustal stretching account for the variety of volcanic styles observed in rift zones.\n\n**Intraplate Volcanism and Hotspots**\n\nSome volcanoes form away from plate boundaries through intraplate volcanism. This is typically attributed to mantle plumes\u2014columns of hot, buoyant rock rising from deep within the mantle, possibly originating near the core\u2013mantle boundary or the lower mantle. When a mantle plume reaches the base of the lithosphere (the rigid outer layer of the Earth), it can cause localized melting and create a hotspot, a region of persistent volcanic activity not directly associated with plate boundaries. As a tectonic plate moves over a stationary hotspot, a chain of volcanoes can form, such as the Hawaiian Islands (Wilson, 1963).\n\nWhile the mantle plume theory is widely accepted, alternative explanations for hotspots include shallow mantle convection or lithospheric extension, which suggest that hotspots may result from processes in the upper mantle or stretching of the lithosphere rather than deep mantle plumes (Anderson, 2000). Evidence for the mantle plume model includes the age progression of volcanic chains and geochemical signatures, while critics point to the lack of clear seismic imaging of plumes and the existence of hotspots in regions of known lithospheric extension. Despite ongoing debate, mantle plumes remain the most common model for intraplate volcanism.\n\n**Transform Boundaries and Volcanism**\n\nUnlike divergent and convergent boundaries, transform boundaries\u2014where two tectonic plates slide past one another horizontally\u2014rarely produce volcanoes. Transform boundaries, such as the San Andreas Fault, do not typically provide a pathway for magma to reach the surface because there is little crustal thinning (which allows magma to rise) or subduction (which generates melt through water addition). As a result, volcanic activity is rare in these settings, though exceptions exist where transform faults intersect with other tectonic features or where local extension occurs (Sykes, 1978).\n\n**Key Geological Terms**\n\n- **Magma chamber:** A subsurface reservoir of molten rock beneath a volcano.\n- **Mantle wedge:** The region of the mantle above a subducting tectonic plate, where melting often occurs due to water released from the subducting slab.\n- **Lithosphere:** The rigid outer layer of the Earth, comprising the crust and uppermost mantle.\n\n**Summary**\n\nIn summary, volcanoes exhibit diverse forms and eruption styles that are closely tied to their tectonic settings: shield volcanoes and cinder cones are common at divergent boundaries and rift zones, stratovolcanoes dominate at convergent boundaries, and hotspots produce chains of shield volcanoes within plates. Understanding the geological processes and settings that give rise to these volcano types\u2014whether at plate boundaries, rift zones, or intraplate hotspots\u2014helps explain the global distribution and behavior of volcanoes.\n\n**References**\n\n- Anderson, D.L. (2000). The thermal state of the upper mantle; No role for mantle plumes. Geophysical Research Letters, 27(22), 3623-3626.\n- Ebinger, C.J. (2005). Continental break-up: the East African perspective. Astronomy & Geophysics, 46(2), 2.16-2.21.\n- Francis, P., & Oppenheimer, C. (2004). Volcanoes. Oxford University Press.\n- Hickson, C.J., & Souther, J.G. (1984). The Wells Gray-Clearwater volcanic field, east-central British Columbia. Canadian Journal of Earth Sciences, 21(3), 267-277.\n- IAVCEI. (n.d.). https://www.iavceivolcano.org/\n- Macdonald, G.A. (1972). Volcanoes. Prentice-Hall.\n- Simkin, T., & Siebert, L. (1994). Volcanoes of the World. Geoscience Press.\n- Sykes, L.R. (1978). Intraplate seismicity, reactivation of preexisting zones of weakness, alkaline magmatism, and other tectonism postdating continental fragmentation. Reviews of Geophysics, 16(4), 621-688.\n- Wilson, J.T. (1963). A possible origin of the Hawaiian Islands. Canadian Journal of Physics, 41(6), 863-870.\n- Wood, C.A. (1980). Morphometric evolution of cinder cones. Journal of Volcanology and Geothermal Research, 7(3-4), 387-413."}}